  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Integrated Psychosocial Care Plan for Comorbid Depression in Breast Cancer Patients in Low Resources Settings</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">1: Determine the clinical and cost-effectiveness of: 1) low intensity guided self-help; 2) moving on ABC Plus, and: 3) Moving on ABC Plus antidepressants using Sequential, Multiple Assignment, Randomized Trial (SMART) in Pakistan (WS1, WS4) 
2: Developing an implementation plan for future scale-up (WS2) 
3: Undertake community and social media campaigns for prevention and early identification of breast cancer (WS3) 
4: Working with policy makers and other stakeholders for policy implementation (WS5) 
5: Build research and clinical capacity and capability to facilitate the scalable and sustainable country-wide provision of mental healthcare among women with breast cancer (WS6) 
6: To develop breast cancer registry using the WHO standardisation guidance and implement across the province of Sindh (WS7)</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Background and Research Question:
Worldwide breast cancer is the leading cause of death in females and affected 2.3 women in 2022. Annual mortality rates are
almost double within south Asian women compared to high-income countries. Co-morbid depression and anxiety is reported in more than
one third of breast-cancer survivors and predict higher recurrence and poorer survival among these women.
Pakistan has one of the highest rates of breast cancer within South Asia. There is a significant delay in first contact with health
professionals due to poor health literacy, stigma, taboos in seeking care for breast health and denial among women and families.
Global Breast Cancer Initiative calls for an integrated psychosocial and physical care plan within health systems especially within
low resource settings.
There is equipoise as to which integrated psychological interventions are most effective and scalable for breast cancer survivors 
in low-income settings to address co-morbid mental health conditions among this population.

Aim &amp; Objectives: We develop and evaluate the effectiveness and implementation of culturally adapted integrated psychosocial
care for depressed breast-cancer survivors across low-resource settings.

Methods and timeline: Research plan consist of seven overlapping work-streams (WSs).
1. Using a three-phase Sequential, Multiple Assignment, Randomised Trial (SMART) in Pakistan
based across health facilities, we evaluate both the clinical and cost-effectiveness of an integrated three-stage adaptive intervention. These being a (1) low intensity guided
self-help,  (2) cognitive behaviour therapy-based Moving on ABC Plus, and moving on ABC Plus with adjuvant antidepressants.
Primary outcome will be depression remission at 48 weeks, measured by Hamilton Depression Rating Scale. Outcomes
assessed at baseline, weeks 6,18,30, and 48 post enrolment (0-60 months)
2. Embedded alongside will be the development of an implementation plan for future scale-up of the evidence-based
approach (0-60 months).
3. Prevention and early identification of breast cancer will be achieved using community and social media campaigns ( 0-60
months).
4. In order to inform policy and practice, working with policy makers and other stakeholders for policy implementation (0-60
months).
5. To build research and clinical capacity in Pakistan and partner low and middle income countries: Six PhD and10 MPhil students along
with training for large number of early career researchers in implementation research and psychosocial
care planning for depression and breast cancer (4-60 months)
6. Develop a breast cancer registry, based on the WHO guidance, for Sind province (0-60 months)</narrative>
  </description>
